    description:
      "Models and data on housing careers show that the value of owning rises with the expected length of stay because transaction costs are large and paid at each move, and that households with little equity are the ones most exposed when prices fall — they cannot trade up, and they are far less likely to move at all, even for a better job. Owner-occupied housing also hedges rent risk, a benefit that accrues only to those who stay. The boundary: the seven-year rule of thumb is a planning heuristic, not a finding; the research says 'longer is better' and 'thin equity is dangerous', and the right horizon depends on local transaction costs and price volatility.",
    action: "Before you buy, write down the probability you will still want this house in seven years — job, family size, school, aging parents — and if it is under half, keep renting or buy something you would be content to stay in. Put down enough that a 10 percent price decline would not leave you underwater, so the decision to move stays yours.",
